<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Saving for Future Goals! – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Personal Success Story</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Saving for Future Goals!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze opinion editorials on general interest topics. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze opinion pieces on personal success, identifying main arguments (e.g., "If young adults start planning their finances early, they will build a strong foundation for future success.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can compare persuasive language in articles on financial and career planning, identifying different viewpoints (e.g., "One article argues that savings should be prioritized, while another suggests investing small amounts monthly for long-term growth.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can discuss editorial viewpoints on success strategies (e.g., "The author uses personal stories, such as how he/she managed his/her finances carefully and saved for future goals, to promote responsible spending.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various financial goals (e.g., a university campus, a house, a small business logo, a travel destination). Students are asked to read the captions and identify which goals they find most appealing and why. Teacher asks, "What personal success stories involve these types of goals?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a short text for main ideas and keywords related to financial planning. For instance, the teacher reads aloud a short paragraph containing key vocabulary words like 'savings', 'investment', 'goals', 'finances', stressing content words (e.g., "You need to PLAN your FINANCES carefully to BUILD strong SAVINGS"). The teacher demonstrates using context clues to infer meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main idea of an 'opinion editorial'?" (Expected answer: It's an article where the author shares their point of view on a topic.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How is a 'persuasive piece' different from just giving information?" (Expected answer: It tries to convince you to believe something or do something.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher provides examples of persuasive language, like strong adjectives ("essential," "significant") or rhetorical questions, and checks for understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Personal Success Story")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive two short opinion editorials (approximately 250-300 words each, B1.3 level) on the theme "Saving for Future Goals!" and the scenario "My Personal Success Story." Both articles discuss different strategies for achieving financial success (e.g., Article A: Prioritizing immediate savings; Article B: Early small investments for long-term growth). The register is semi-formal, persuasive, and informative. The articles incorporate the target vocabulary (e.g., 'achievements', 'investment', 'career', 'strategies', 'future', 'opportunities', 'manage finances', 'build success', 'long-term goals') and grammar (Present Perfect, Future Continuous, First Conditional).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student analyzes opinion editorials on general interest topics and persuasive pieces on current events, identifying main arguments and viewpoints.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read both articles quickly to identify the main topic of each. They match each article with a summary statement (e.g., "This article focuses on the importance of an early savings plan," "This article discusses the benefits of investing small amounts over time.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False/Not Mentioned:</b> Students read specific statements about each article (e.g., "The author of Article A has saved some money for their college studies.", "In five years, young adults will be managing their own companies if they start investing now.", "If I continue my studies, I will find more opportunities for career growth.") and mark them as True, False, or Not Mentioned, requiring them to locate specific details and identify arguments.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Analyzing main arguments and persuasive language; identifying present perfect, future continuous, and first conditional structures in context; practicing vocabulary related to financial planning and success.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Argument Identification:</b> Students work individually to complete a graphic organizer for each article. For Article A, they identify 3-4 main arguments supporting early savings using target vocabulary (e.g., "The author highlights the 'essential' need to 'save' money for future 'goals'. If young adults 'manage' their 'finances' carefully, they 'will build' a strong foundation for future 'success'."). For Article B, they identify arguments for early investment. They also highlight sentences that use Present Perfect ("I have saved some money for my college studies."), Future Continuous ("In five years, I will be managing my own company."), and First Conditional ("If I continue my studies, I will find more opportunities.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Persuasive Language Analysis:</b> In pairs, students re-read sections of the articles. They are given a list of adjectives and verbs from the vocabulary list (e.g., 'impactful', 'essential', 'significant', 'aspire', 'succeed', 'contribute') and identify where these words are used persuasively in the articles. They underline sentences containing these words and discuss how they make the author's argument stronger. For example, "The author uses 'essential' to make saving sound non-negotiable."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Viewpoint Comparison:</b> Students work in pairs to compare the viewpoints presented in the two articles using a Venn diagram or a comparison chart. They identify points of agreement and disagreement regarding the best 'strategies' for financial 'growth' and 'achievements'. They share one sentence where the author uses a personal story to promote a specific strategy. This promotes respectful discussion on personal achievements and goals.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Personal Success Story")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary and Analysis Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students will select one of the opinion editorials or a blend of both. They will prepare a short (2-3 minute) "editorial review" where they present the main arguments, identify the author's viewpoint on "Saving for Future Goals!", and point out examples of persuasive language used. They must use at least three vocabulary words (e.g., 'investment', 'strategy', 'opportunities', 'goals') and one grammatical structure (Present Perfect, Future Continuous, or First Conditional) from the lesson to describe or analyze the text. For example, "The author argues that if we 'invest' early, we 'will be managing' greater 'growth' in the 'future'." Each group will verbally present their analysis, focusing on how the author promotes responsible spending or career planning, demonstrating respectful discussion of goals and aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in analyzing text, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to practice stressing content words for clear communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations and a short written comprehension check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Presentation:</b> Teacher uses a checklist to observe each group's ability to clearly present the main arguments, identify the author's viewpoint, and use persuasive language examples from the articles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual Written Comprehension:</b> Students complete a short written task answering questions such as: "What is one key argument from Article A that convinced you about 'saving' early?" and "Identify one sentence from Article B that uses persuasive language, and explain why it's persuasive."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in identifying main arguments and viewpoints.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification of persuasive language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'strategies', 'finances', 'goals', 'investment').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammatical structures (Present Perfect, Future Continuous, First Conditional).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative participation in group work.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new strategy for 'saving for future goals' I learned from today's articles is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easiest/most challenging to (identify main arguments / compare viewpoints / find persuasive language) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-6 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has analyzing these opinion pieces helped me better understand different 'strategies' for achieving 'personal success' and 'financial growth'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading skill did I use effectively today, and what skill do I need to 'improve' for future reading tasks?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students analyze the articles and identify persuasive language? The structured graphic organizers for arguments were effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled to articulate their comparison of viewpoints verbally; perhaps providing more sentence starters for comparison would be beneficial for the speaking activity next lesson. Emphasize practicing content word stress more explicitly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will engage in a debate or structured discussion based on the arguments and viewpoints analyzed today, using the vocabulary and grammar to express their own 'goals' and 'aspirations' regarding 'saving for future goals!'</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>